CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:1.1.t72-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:1.1.t105
REASONING=The sentence has one independent verb, **video** (t92), which governs an indirect statement anchored by the infinitive **exstitisse** (t104), with **hunc** (t91) as its accusative subject and **principem** (t94) as predicate noun (exsisto here functions as a linking verb, "turned out to be my leader"). The **quoad** clause (t73–t86) is a dependent clause: *quoad* is the subordinating conjunction pointing at *video*, and **potest** (t75) is its verb, with *mens mea* as subject and two coordinated complementary infinitives, **respicere** (t78) and **recordari** (t85), joined by *et* (t82). Complementary infinitives are not themselves verbal expressions, so they get no `verbalunits` entry; each keeps its own object (*spatium*, *memoriam*) as if finite. *praeteriti* modifies *temporis*, which is a genitive on *spatium*; *pueritiae* is a genitive on *memoriam*, and *ultimam* is adjectival with *memoriam*. **longissime** (t74): I attach it adverbially to *potest*, though it could plausibly be read as modifying *respicere* ("look back as far as possible"). The idiom *quoad longissime potest* ("as far back as it possibly can") makes the modal verb the more natural head, so I chose *potest*; this is a genuine close call. **repetens** (t90) is a predicate/circumstantial participle agreeing with the unexpressed subject ("I") of *video*, so I add an implied-subject token (1.t89_implied) taking the 'subject' relation into *video*, with *repetens* relating to it via 'circumstantial participle'. I treat *repetens* as intransitive here ("tracing back from that point"), since it has no expressed object; *inde* and *usque* are adverbs modifying it. **nam** opens the sentence with no verb to its left to pair with, so it takes only relatedtoken1 -> *video*. The two *ad*-phrases form the "et A et B" polysyndeton shape: the first *et* (t95) is introductory (only relatedtoken1 -> t96, the first *ad*), the second *et* (t98) relates to both prepositions (t96, t99). *suscipiendam* and *ingrediendam* are gerundives, hence plain adjectivals agreeing with **rationem**, which is the object of the (nearer, second) preposition *ad*. *horum studiorum* is a genitive on *rationem*.

